{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"emblematic-mutinous-loris","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context\n\nS1 produces a rewritten below-horizon baseline on a shadow clone. This story builds the\n**publish-swap** that would replace the published `tickets` history with that baseline — as a\nper-backend adapter over one precondition (\"replace iff the remote is unchanged since T0, else\nabort+retry\") — plus the rollback re-convergence design. It STOPS before executing any real swap.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **git ref remotes** (bare/GitHub/Gerrit): swap via `git push --force-with-lease=refs/heads/tickets:T0` (old→new ref CAS).\n- **S3 `git-remote-s3` backend (ADR 0093):** there is NO atomic ref CAS. The helper stores one git bundle per push, and the non-interactive auto-doctor (`src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py`) folds divergent heads by lossless 2-parent merges and NEVER discards a head — so a normally-pushed rewrite would be MERGED BACK (resurrected). Implement an EXCLUSIVE offline bundle-set replacement under the per-ref push-lock: assert the head set equals T0 (the CAS precondition), back up the bundle set to a rollback key prefix, write ONE rewritten bundle, delete the originals — bypassing the auto-doctor.\n- **Rollback re-convergence (plan-review advisory #4):** design and document the path for clients that already reconciled onto the rewritten baseline (new SHAs) BEFORE a rollback — after rollback they point at a baseline graph the remote no longer has. Specify how they re-converge (e.g. treat the restored pre-reclaim ref as authoritative and force-override per the C3 stale-clone protocol).\n\n## Out of scope\n\nExecuting a swap against any real `tickets` remote — that is a separate human go/no-go after the S3\ndry-run. Everything here is validated against throwaway remotes/fixtures.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The git-ref adapter swaps via `--force-with-lease` and ABORTS (no write) when the remote moved since T0, verified on a throwaway bare remote.\n- [ ] The S3 adapter performs an exclusive push-locked bundle-set replacement that asserts the T0 head set, backs up bundles to a rollback prefix, writes one rewritten bundle, deletes originals, and does NOT trigger an auto-doctor merge — verified on a fake-S3 fixture.\n- [ ] A backup ref / rollback bundle prefix is captured before the first destructive step in each adapter.\n- [ ] The rollback re-convergence path for already-reconciled clients is documented and covered by a reconvergence fixture (a client on the rewritten baseline is force-overridden back onto the restored ref).\n- [ ] [non-codebase] No real `tickets` remote is swapped by this story.\n\n## Portability\n\nBoth adapters use either core-git ref primitives or the store's existing S3 seam; no single-CI-provider dependence.","id":"4b2a-3ae8-14ee-463a","parent_id":"536b-8930-b922-4063","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S2: per-backend publish-swap (git-ref CAS + S3 bundle replacement) + rollback re-convergence"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788046507255058770,"uuid":"08693829-cef6-4b88-a427-3e990c4a518a"}